VALUES (
  '2026/06/05/ceasefire-recency-bias-reversal',
  'behaviour', 'article',
  'The Investors Who Sold Into the Shock Are Now Buying Into the Relief',
  'The Israel-Lebanon ceasefire is inverting the recency bias that geopolitical volatility built over three months. Advisors who understand the mechanism can stop the second mistake before the first one is even recovered.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>Recency bias now runs in both directions.</strong><span> Three months of escalation conditioned retail investors to treat bad geopolitical news as permanent. The June 4 Israel-Lebanon ceasefire is now triggering the mirror error: treating a fragile diplomatic signal as a resolved outcome.</span></li>
<li><strong>The availability heuristic is loaded with oil shock data.</strong><span> For investors who have watched energy prices surge since March 4, the dominant memory is disruption. A single ceasefire does not overwrite that memory, but it activates hope, and hope is where the second costly mistake often originates.</span></li>
<li><strong>Kahneman and Tversky''s loss aversion framework explains both moves.</strong><span> Investors sold into the shock to avoid further losses. They may now chase recovery gains to "get back to even," a classic manifestation of the break-even effect described in prospect theory.</span></li>
<li><strong>WTI fell roughly 3% on June 4 after the ceasefire announcement.</strong><span> Brent is trading near $95. The strait remains closed. The physical supply disruption has not changed, only the probability distribution of future outcomes has shifted.</span></li>
<li><strong>The advisory opportunity is precise and time-sensitive.</strong><span> Clients who sold in March through May are now facing the decision of when to re-enter. The advisor who frames this moment correctly prevents a second round of timing error.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>When markets fall sharply on geopolitical news, investors make one predictable mistake: they sell into the shock at the moment of maximum uncertainty. Kahneman and Tversky identified the mechanism in 1979. Loss aversion makes the pain of a potential further loss feel more urgent than the cost of locking in the existing one. The result is a decision made under peak emotional load, at exactly the wrong moment in the price cycle.</p>

<p>What behavioural finance has studied less thoroughly is what happens next: when the geopolitical signal reverses, even partially, the same investors face a symmetric temptation. Three months of conditioning to a single pattern, escalation follows escalation, produces a recency bias that has now been abruptly inverted. The Israel-Lebanon ceasefire announced June 4 did not resolve the Strait of Hormuz closure. It shifted the probability distribution of outcomes. That is a meaningful distinction. The market, and the retail investors watching it, often does not make it.</p>

<h2>What Recency Bias Built in Three Months</h2>

<p>Since the Hormuz closure began March 4, Canadian retail investors have experienced a consistent pattern: geopolitical headlines worsen, oil prices rise, equity markets wobble, and the most recent data point is always bad. The availability heuristic, the cognitive shortcut Kahneman described in which the most easily recalled events feel most probable, has been loaded with three months of supply disruption, price spikes, and ceasefire failures.</p>

<p>This conditioning is not irrational. It is how human cognition is designed to function: recent experience is weighted heavily because, in most contexts, it is the most relevant predictor of near-term outcomes. The problem is that geopolitical crises do not follow the same mean-reversion patterns as economic cycles. A ceasefire after a prolonged conflict is genuinely discontinuous, not a return to a trend.</p>

<p>The result is that investors who adapted their reflexes to a deteriorating environment are now facing a signal that their adapted reflexes have not been trained to process. The availability heuristic points to disruption. The new data point points toward resolution. The cognitive dissonance between these two frames is precisely where poor decisions are made.</p>

<h2>The Break-Even Effect and the Re-Entry Trap</h2>

<p>Prospect theory describes a phenomenon called the break-even effect: investors who have experienced losses are willing to accept elevated risk in order to return to their reference point, the price at which they originally held. For clients who reduced equity or energy exposure in March or April, the reference point is the portfolio level before the Hormuz shock. The ceasefire signal now activates the psychological drive to recover that ground.</p>

<p>Shefrin and Statman documented the disposition effect, the tendency to sell winners too early and hold losers too long, in part as an expression of the same reference-point psychology. The re-entry trap is the disposition effect operating in the opposite direction: rather than holding a loser in hopes of recovering, the investor chases a rally in a position they previously exited, now at a higher price, to satisfy the psychological need to be made whole.</p>

<p>The trap is compounded by the nature of this particular ceasefire. The Strait of Hormuz remains closed. WTI fell roughly 3% on June 4 to the low-to-mid $90s, but that move reflects a shift in forward probability, not a restoration of supply. The Bank of Canada noted in its April 29 Monetary Policy Report that its baseline forecast assumes Brent gradually declines from $90 in Q2. A ceasefire that does not reopen the strait does not accelerate that path. The client who re-enters energy at $95 Brent on a ceasefire signal may be buying the hope, not the resolution.</p>

<h2>The Reinforcement Pattern Advisors Must Interrupt</h2>

<p>Barber and Odean''s research on retail investor trading behaviour established that the most damaging trading pattern is not any single decision but the reinforcement loop: a bad decision is followed by a reactive correction that is also poorly timed, which establishes a pattern of systematic underperformance against the index. The investor who sold Canadian energy names in April and now buys back after the ceasefire rally has potentially executed two consecutively poorly timed trades.</p>

<p>The three-month Hormuz disruption has created conditions for this loop to run at scale. Investors who sold were arguably responding rationally to uncertainty. The question is whether the uncertainty has actually resolved, or whether the market has mispriced a fragile diplomatic signal as a durable outcome. ING analysts noted Thursday that the physical oil market is tightening every day the strait remains closed regardless of ceasefire status. Goldman Sachs has flagged that refined product inventories, particularly jet fuel and naphtha, are being drawn down at an accelerating rate. The ceasefire improves the probability distribution, but the underlying supply disruption continues.</p>

<p>WTI''s price path over the past 13 weeks illustrates the volatility embedded in ceasefire oscillation. Starting at approximately $70 pre-conflict, the benchmark surged above $106 at peak escalation in early May, then pulled back into the $90s during ceasefire windows, only to surge again on renewed hostilities. Each of these moves has generated a re-entry signal that has subsequently been reversed. The advisor who helps a client see this pattern before the client acts on the latest signal is delivering a service that no market data feed can replicate.</p>

<div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
<div style="background:#f5f5f5;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d0d0;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
<span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#111;letter-spacing:0.02em;">WTI CRUDE OIL</span>
<span style="font-size:20px;font-weight:700;color:#111;">~$91-94</span>
<span style="font-size:13px;color:#c0392b;">▼ ~3% June 4</span>
<span style="font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-left:auto;">Weekly &nbsp;|&nbsp; Mar 4 to Jun 5, 2026</span>
</div>
<div style="padding:12px 14px 8px;">
<script>
(function(){
  var data = [
    {w:"Mar 4",v:70.2},{w:"Mar 9",v:78.5},{w:"Mar 16",v:84.1},{w:"Mar 23",v:89.4},
    {w:"Mar 30",v:92.8},{w:"Apr 6",v:97.9},{w:"Apr 13",v:91.2},{w:"Apr 20",v:83.9},
    {w:"Apr 27",v:89.6},{w:"May 4",v:102.3},{w:"May 11",v:98.7},{w:"May 18",v:92.2},
    {w:"May 25",v:94.8},{w:"Jun 1",v:97.1},{w:"Jun 5",v:92.6}
  ];
  var n = data.length;
  var margin = {left:54,right:24,top:18,bottom:46};
  var W=680,H=300;
  var PW=W-margin.left-margin.right, PH=H-margin.top-margin.bottom;
  var svg = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","svg");
  svg.setAttribute("viewBox","0 0 "+W+" "+H);
  svg.setAttribute("xmlns","http://www.w3.org/2000/svg");
  function el(tag,attrs){var e=document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg",tag);for(var k in attrs)e.setAttribute(k,attrs[k]);return e;}
  function txt(s,attrs){var e=el("text",attrs);e.textContent=s;return e;}
  var vals=data.map(function(d){return d.v;});
  var minV=Math.min.apply(null,vals)-4, maxV=Math.max.apply(null,vals)+4;
  function xS(i){return margin.left+i*(PW/(n-1));}
  function yS(v){return margin.top+PH-(v-minV)/(maxV-minV)*PH;}
  // Gridlines
  var yTicks=[70,80,90,100,110];
  yTicks.forEach(function(v){
    if(v<minV||v>maxV)return;
    svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:margin.left,x2:margin.left+PW,y1:yS(v),y2:yS(v),stroke:"#ececec","stroke-width":"0.5"}));
    svg.appendChild(txt("$"+v,{x:margin.left-6,y:yS(v)+3,"text-anchor":"end","font-size":"8.5","fill":"#aaa","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
  });
  // Ceasefire band April 17-27
  var x1=xS(7), x2=xS(8);
  var band=el("rect",{x:x1,y:margin.top,width:x2-x1,height:PH,fill:"#2e7d32","fill-opacity":"0.07"});
  svg.appendChild(band);
  svg.appendChild(txt("CEASEFIRE 1",{x:(x1+x2)/2,y:margin.top+10,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"7","font-weight":"700","fill":"#2e7d32","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
  // Event marker: Hormuz peak escalation ~May 4
  var ex1=xS(9);
  svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:ex1,x2:ex1,y1:margin.top,y2:margin.top+PH,stroke:"#1a3560","stroke-dasharray":"2,3","stroke-width":"1","stroke-opacity":"0.5"}));
  svg.appendChild(txt("UAE",{x:ex1+3,y:margin.top+12,"font-size":"7","font-weight":"700","fill":"#1a3560","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
  svg.appendChild(txt("attacks",{x:ex1+3,y:margin.top+21,"font-size":"7","font-weight":"700","fill":"#1a3560","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
  // Event marker: Israel-Lebanon ceasefire June 4
  var ex2=xS(14);
  svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:ex2,x2:ex2,y1:margin.top,y2:margin.top+PH,stroke:"#2e7d32","stroke-dasharray":"2,3","stroke-width":"1","stroke-opacity":"0.6"}));
  svg.appendChild(txt("IL-LB",{x:ex2-5,y:margin.top+12,"text-anchor":"end","font-size":"7","font-weight":"700","fill":"#2e7d32","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
  svg.appendChild(txt("ceasefire",{x:ex2-5,y:margin.top+21,"text-anchor":"end","font-size":"7","font-weight":"700","fill":"#2e7d32","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
  // $70 pre-conflict reference line
  var refY=yS(70.2);
  svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:margin.left,x2:margin.left+PW,y1:refY,y2:refY,stroke:"#7a3030","stroke-dasharray":"3,3","stroke-width":"1"}));
  svg.appendChild(txt("Pre-conflict $70",{x:650,y:refY-3,"text-anchor":"end","font-size":"7.5","fill":"#7a3030","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
  // Line path
  var d="M";
  data.forEach(function(pt,i){d+=(i?""+(xS(i))+","+(yS(pt.v)):" "+xS(0)+","+yS(pt.v)+"L");});
  // fix path
  var pathD="M "+xS(0)+","+yS(data[0].v);
  for(var i=1;i<n;i++)pathD+=" L "+xS(i)+","+yS(data[i].v);
  svg.appendChild(el("path",{d:pathD,fill:"none",stroke:"#4a5568","stroke-width":"1.8"}));
  // Gold pill on current value
  var lastX=xS(n-1), lastY=yS(data[n-1].v);
  var pillW=58,pillH=16;
  var pillX=lastX-pillW-6;
  var pillY=lastY-pillH/2;
  svg.appendChild(el("circle",{cx:lastX,cy:lastY,r:4,fill:"#4a5568"}));
  svg.appendChild(el("rect",{x:pillX,y:pillY,width:pillW,height:pillH,rx:"3",fill:"#e8a825"}));
  svg.appendChild(txt("$92.6",{x:pillX+pillW/2,y:pillY+pillH/2+4,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"9","font-weight":"700","fill":"#111","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
  // MA overlay (5-week)
  var maPath="";
  for(var i=4;i<n;i++){
    var sum=0;for(var j=i-4;j<=i;j++)sum+=data[j].v;
    var ma=sum/5;
    maPath+=(i===4?"M ":"L ")+xS(i)+","+yS(ma)+" ";
  }
  svg.appendChild(el("path",{d:maPath,fill:"none",stroke:"#888888","stroke-width":"1","stroke-dasharray":"4,3"}));
  svg.appendChild(txt("5-wk MA",{x:xS(n-1)-4,y:yS(data[n-1].v)+22,"text-anchor":"end","font-size":"7.5","fill":"#888","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
  // X-axis labels every 3rd
  data.forEach(function(d,i){
    if(i%3===0){
      svg.appendChild(txt(d.w,{x:xS(i),y:H-margin.bottom+14,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"8","fill":"#999","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"}));
    }
  });
  var container=document.currentScript?document.currentScript.parentNode:null;
  if(container)container.appendChild(svg);
})();
</script>
</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: CNBC, Reuters, Trading Economics, WTI spot price data, March 4 to June 5, 2026. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">WTI price path from the Hormuz closure through four ceasefire oscillations, showing the break-even level investors who sold in March are chasing, and the current level after the June 4 Israel-Lebanon ceasefire pulled prices approximately 3% lower. The strait remains closed; the supply disruption is intact.</p>

<p>The chart above tracks exactly the pattern Barber and Odean documented in retail trading studies: each relief rally triggers re-entry, each escalation triggers exit, and the net result of timing these swings is systematic underperformance relative to holding. The investor who sold at $70 pre-conflict and is considering re-entry at $92 after a ceasefire is not recovering their position. They are taking on new risk at a higher price, based on a diplomatic signal that has failed four times in three months.</p>

<h2>What the Advisor Knows That the Client Does Not</h2>

<p>The advisor''s informational edge in this environment is not access to better data. It is the ability to distinguish between a change in probability distribution and a change in underlying reality. The Israel-Lebanon ceasefire is the former. The Strait of Hormuz remains closed. Tanker traffic through the strait is light, as Reuters reported Thursday. Goldman Sachs has noted that global oil inventories are not yet at critically low levels but that refined product buffers are being depleted rapidly. The IMF has estimated the war has disrupted approximately 14 million barrels per day of global production.</p>

<p>None of this changes because Israel and Lebanon agreed to stop firing at each other. What changes is the forward probability of a broader US-Iran resolution, which is genuinely meaningful. But meaningful is not the same as certain, and it is not the same as imminent. The Fitch Ratings downgrade of its 2026 global growth forecast by 0.2 percentage points, to 2.4%, reflects the cumulative economic cost of a disruption that a diplomatic signal has not yet ended.</p>

<p>The advisor who communicates this distinction concisely, to a client who is experiencing the emotional pull of the break-even effect, is doing the most important work in portfolio management. Not stock selection. Not asset allocation optimization. The prevention of the second poor timing decision in a sequence that began with the first.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who reduced equity or energy exposure during the March-May escalation period are experiencing cautious optimism alongside an undercurrent of urgency. The ceasefire feels like permission to "fix" the decisions they have been second-guessing for weeks. Underneath the optimism is the break-even psychology: the drive to return to where they were before the shock, not necessarily to make the objectively correct next move. Clients who held through the volatility feel validated and may be tempted toward overconfidence in their thesis. Both emotional states present advisory risk in opposite directions.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">What happened yesterday is genuinely significant. An Israel-Lebanon ceasefire improves the chances of a broader resolution. I want to make sure we''re responding to the right thing, though. The Strait of Hormuz is still closed. The physical oil supply disruption that started in March has not changed. What changed is the probability that it ends sooner rather than later. Those are different things, and they call for different responses. If we add back energy exposure today, we''re buying a ceasefire signal, not a resolution. We''ve seen three of those signals since March, and each one has partially reversed. I''m not saying we do nothing. I''m saying we should be precise about what we''re actually responding to, and build a plan that works across the range of outcomes, not just the best one.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients who reduced energy exposure in March or April and are now reconsidering re-entry. The break-even effect is operating strongly in this group; they need a structured re-entry framework, not a green light.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients who held through the volatility. They need reinforcement that the decision to hold was correct, with a clear update on what has and has not changed in the underlying situation.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with diversified mandates and no energy overweight. This group has avoided the timing problem entirely and the ceasefire signal is primarily noise for their portfolio construction.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify every client who reduced energy or equity exposure between March 4 and May 31 and flag them for proactive outreach today.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review each flagged client''s current cash position and the entry point at which they reduced exposure versus current market prices.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a one-paragraph note summarizing what the ceasefire did and did not change, for use in client communications.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document all client conversations today with explicit notation of the behavioural framing provided, for compliance and continuity purposes.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review your energy sector holdings across all books for clients holding above-benchmark weights, in anticipation of further ceasefire-driven price moves.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What the ceasefire does and does not change for your portfolio<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I wanted to follow up on yesterday''s Israel-Lebanon ceasefire announcement and give you a clear picture of what it means for your portfolio.<br><br>
The ceasefire is meaningful. It increases the probability that the broader US-Iran conflict moves toward resolution, which would ultimately lead to the Strait of Hormuz reopening and oil prices declining from current levels. That is a positive development.<br><br>
What has not changed: the strait is still closed, and the supply disruption that has driven oil prices from approximately $70 to the low-to-mid $90s since March is still in place. Markets are pricing an improved probability distribution, not an actual resolution.<br><br>
We have seen similar ceasefire signals in April and May, each of which provided temporary price relief before renewed tensions. I am not dismissing the significance of this one; the Lebanon element is new and potentially more durable. But I want us to be precise about what we are responding to before making any portfolio changes.<br><br>
I will be in touch with a specific recommendation for your situation once I have had a chance to review the data from this morning''s Canadian jobs report and assess the broader picture heading into the Bank of Canada decision next Wednesday.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors in energy-heavy portfolios:</strong> Retail investors who have been managing their own exposure to Canadian energy names without professional guidance are the most vulnerable to the ceasefire re-entry trap. Many reduced exposure during the April-May period and are now watching the relief rally with no framework for distinguishing a probability shift from a resolution.</p>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors who held through the volatility:</strong> Investors who held without a plan are now overweight energy relative to their original mandate without having made a conscious decision. They need portfolio review and a framework for what they actually own.</p>
<p><strong>Recent retirees in drawdown:</strong> Clients in the early drawdown phase who experienced significant portfolio volatility since March may have cash positions that need to be thoughtfully redeployed.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling because the ceasefire news yesterday is the kind of signal that tends to prompt portfolio decisions, and I wanted to offer a perspective on what it actually means before you act on it.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The most expensive decisions retail investors make are not during the obvious crisis moments. They are made in the ambiguous recovery windows, when a partial positive signal looks like full resolution. That is exactly where we are today. A ceasefire that does not reopen the Strait of Hormuz is not the same as an end to the supply disruption that has driven oil prices up 30% since March.</p>
<p>An advisor who can hold that distinction steady, under client pressure to act, is delivering a form of risk management that no index fund or self-directed platform provides. The DIY investor watching the relief rally has no one to tell them the difference. You do.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How has your portfolio held up over the last three months with everything happening in the Middle East?</p>
<p>Did you make any changes to your energy or equity positions after the Hormuz closure started in March?</p>
<p>With the ceasefire news yesterday, are you thinking about adding back any positions you reduced?</p>
<p>What is your sense of how long the current disruption might last, and how are you planning for the different outcomes?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The ceasefire and what it actually means for your energy holdings<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
Yesterday''s Israel-Lebanon ceasefire moved oil prices down roughly 3% and created what feels like a green light for investors who reduced energy exposure over the past three months.<br><br>
I want to offer a different frame: the Strait of Hormuz is still closed. The physical supply disruption has not changed. What changed is the probability that a broader resolution happens sooner. Those are different things, and they tend to produce different portfolio decisions.<br><br>
If you have been managing your own exposure to Canadian energy names through this period, I would be glad to offer a second opinion on where things stand now and what the range of outcomes looks like from here.<br><br>
[Your Name] | [Firm] | [Phone]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

