<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Monthly payment increase at renewal from a 2% to 4.5% fixed rate across eight mortgage balance levels. The $500K balance, highlighted, represents the median renewal balance in the Toronto CMA according to CMHC 2025 data. Balances above $700K face monthly increases exceeding $940.</p>

<h2>The RRSP and TFSA Dimension</h2>

<p>Clients facing significant mortgage payment increases may have an instinct to reduce registered account contributions in order to absorb the cash flow shock. For advisors, this is a critical inflection point. Allowing a client to reduce TFSA contributions to service a higher mortgage payment is a suboptimal outcome in almost every planning scenario, because the after-tax cost of the mortgage is fixed while the compounding cost of reduced TFSA contribution room lost is permanent.</p>

<p>The more nuanced planning conversation involves clients who hold significant fixed income inside their RRSPs at current yields. A client renewing a mortgage at 4.5% while holding Government of Canada bonds yielding 3.2% inside a registered account is paying a 1.3-percentage-point spread to maintain a fixed income position they could reduce in order to accelerate mortgage repayment. The mathematics of this trade-off depend on marginal tax rates, amortization period, and asset allocation targets, but for many mass-affluent clients in peak earning years, the spread is worth examining before reflexively increasing mortgage amortization to reduce monthly payments.</p>

<p>For incorporated clients holding investment portfolios inside a Canadian-Controlled Private Corporation, the renewal conversation intersects with the capital gains inclusion rate environment. The rate change that took effect June 25, 2025, at two-thirds inclusion above the $250,000 personal threshold, altered the after-tax cost of liquidating corporate investment assets to fund mortgage paydown. Advisors serving this client segment should have updated that analysis in advance of the renewal event rather than reactively.</p>

<h2>The Five-Day Window and What It Actually Requires</h2>

<p>The June 10 BoC decision is a natural client communication trigger. What it requires practically is a client-facing summary of the decision''s implications that goes beyond the overnight rate announcement. The clients who need the most from the June 10 communication are not those watching the macro news. They are those who received a renewal letter from their lender two months ago, have not yet responded, and are about to automatically roll over at a posted rate that is 40 to 80 basis points above what a negotiated or brokered renewal would produce.</p>

<p>John Webster, former CEO of Scotia Mortgage Authority, has noted publicly that banks rely on client inertia at renewal: most clients sign the renewal letter without negotiating. For a $600,000 mortgage balance, the difference between a posted 4.8% rate and a negotiated 4.3% rate is approximately $170 per month over the renewal term, or more than $10,000 over five years. The advisor who surfaces this before the client signs is delivering a concrete, quantifiable benefit that strengthens the relationship regardless of what the BoC decides on Wednesday.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients facing renewal are experiencing a combination of resignation and anxiety. Many have known this payment increase was coming for months and have been avoiding the full calculation. Some have already received their lender''s renewal offer and found it confusing or alarming. Clients who are also watching energy-driven inflation are feeling squeezed from two directions simultaneously. The emotional state is not panic, but it is suppressed dread, and it often translates into avoidance behaviour: not returning the lender''s call, not asking their advisor about it, hoping it resolves itself.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I want to talk to you about your mortgage renewal before the Bank of Canada decision on Wednesday. The BoC is widely expected to hold, which means the interest rate environment you are renewing into is not going to change materially in the next few days. What can change is whether you get a negotiated rate or a posted rate, and that difference could be worth $150 to $200 a month over five years. There are also a few planning decisions tied to the renewal that we should work through together, specifically around your registered accounts and whether the right response to a higher payment is extending your amortization or adjusting something else in the plan. This is one of those conversations that is worth having before the clock runs out on your renewal window.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with five-year fixed mortgages originated in 2021 or 2022 at rates between 1.5% and 2.5%, particularly those with balances above $500,000 in high-cost markets (Toronto, Vancouver, Calgary). The payment shock is material and immediate.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with variable-rate mortgages already at prime plus spread, whose payments have been adjusting throughout the rate cycle. Their renewal risk is lower but their exposure to a potential June or July hike is new.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients in the early years of their mortgage with significant amortization remaining who are considering accelerated paydown. A higher renewal rate actually strengthens the mathematical case for directing excess cash to principal reduction rather than non-registered investment accounts.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull a list of every client with a mortgage renewal date in the next 90 days and segment by balance and estimated rate delta.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For each renewal client, check whether they hold significant HELOC balances and model the combined carrying cost at current rates.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review RRSP fixed income allocations for clients facing renewal: is the after-tax bond yield-to-mortgage-rate spread worth examining?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For incorporated clients, update the post-inclusion-rate analysis of liquidating corporate investment assets versus extending amortization.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a one-page renewal summary for clients to bring to their lender negotiation, including a recommended rate benchmark based on current broker market.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Your mortgage renewal and the Bank of Canada decision on Wednesday<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
The Bank of Canada announces its rate decision this Wednesday, and I want to give you the relevant context before then, particularly if your mortgage is coming up for renewal in the next few months.<br><br>
The short version: the BoC is widely expected to hold its rate at 2.25%. That does not meaningfully change the fixed mortgage rate environment, which is driven primarily by Government of Canada five-year bond yields rather than the overnight rate. What it does mean is that the window of hoping rates come down before your renewal is effectively closed.<br><br>
The most important thing you can do right now, if you haven''t already, is get at least two or three competitive renewal quotes before responding to your current lender''s offer. The spread between a posted rate and a negotiated broker rate is typically 40 to 80 basis points, which translates to meaningful dollar savings over a five-year term at your balance level.<br><br>
I would also like to review your registered account allocations and any HELOC balances in light of the renewal, as there are planning decisions that interact with each other here. Can we find 30 minutes this week?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Homeowners with 2021-22 fixed mortgages in high-cost markets:</strong> This is the core renewal cohort. Toronto CMA clients with balances above $600,000 represent the highest payment-shock exposure and the clearest planning gap. Many are navigating this entirely without professional guidance.</p>
<p><strong>Incorporated business owners with investment portfolios:</strong> This group faces the most complex interaction between mortgage renewal, CCPC investment account management, and the post-inclusion-rate capital gains environment. The planning value is high and the DIY complexity is prohibitive.</p>
<p><strong>Dual-income couples in peak earning years:</strong> Households with combined income above $200,000 who own a primary residence and potentially a rental property face the most acute cash flow optimization decision at renewal. They need a plan, not a rate.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am reaching out ahead of the Bank of Canada decision on Wednesday because this is the last major data point before mortgage renewal decisions in the next 90 days get locked in, and I want to make sure you have the full picture before anything gets signed.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The mortgage renewal conversation looks simple on the surface: here is your new rate, sign here. The planning underneath it is not simple. Whether to extend amortization, what to do with registered accounts when cash flow tightens, how the renewal interacts with a HELOC, what the capital gains implications are for an incorporated client who is considering liquidating corporate assets to pay down the mortgage: none of these questions have obvious answers, and all of them interact with each other.</p>
<p>A client managing this on their own will almost certainly make at least one suboptimal decision because they are optimizing for one variable when four are in play simultaneously. That is the advisor''s value in this conversation, and it is concrete, quantifiable, and immediately relevant to a very large number of Canadians right now.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Is your mortgage coming up for renewal in the next three to six months?</p>
<p>Have you received a renewal offer from your current lender yet, and have you had a chance to compare it to what the market is offering?</p>
<p>When you think about the payment increase you are likely facing, what is your instinct: absorb it through your monthly cash flow, extend your amortization, or do something else?</p>
<p>Are you carrying any HELOC balances or investment loans alongside your primary mortgage?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Before you sign your mortgage renewal this year<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The Bank of Canada announces its rate decision this Wednesday. Rates are expected to hold, which means the environment for the 980,000 Canadian mortgages renewing this year is essentially set.<br><br>
If your mortgage is coming up for renewal, you are likely looking at a meaningful payment increase compared to what you were paying under your previous term. The spread between a posted bank rate and a negotiated renewal rate is often 40 to 80 basis points, which adds up to real money over five years at a $500,000-plus balance.<br><br>
More importantly, the renewal decision does not exist in isolation. It interacts with your registered accounts, any investment loans you carry, and if you are incorporated, your corporate investment portfolio. Getting those pieces right together is worth a conversation before anything gets signed.<br><br>
I would be glad to walk through your specific situation at no obligation.<br><br>
[Your Name] | [Firm] | [Phone]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

