</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Four Brent crude scenarios bracketing the realistic distribution following the June 4 Israel-Lebanon ceasefire. The vertical dashed line marks the current spot price of approximately $95. The base case mid-point of $90 sits just below current levels; the constructive scenario range begins at $78. Each dot represents the analyst consensus mid-point for that scenario; the shaded bar shows the range.</p>

<h2>The Canadian Portfolio Read-Through</h2>

<p>Canada''s exposure to this situation runs through three channels: energy sector equity valuation, the Canadian dollar, and the Bank of Canada''s inflation path. Each responds differently to the scenario distribution above.</p>

<p>The TSX energy sub-index has broadly tracked WTI through the conflict period, outperforming the broad composite significantly. Suncor, Canadian Natural Resources, Cenovus, and Enbridge have all benefited from elevated oil prices. A resolution scenario that brings Brent to $80 to $88 compresses those valuations materially, particularly for producers with higher breakeven costs. A prolonged-negotiation base case sustains current valuations but removes the upside asymmetry that energy names have carried since March.</p>

<p>The Canadian dollar''s relationship to oil is well-documented but has been partially decoupled in this conflict period by the countervailing effect of risk-off sentiment and CAD-specific economic softness. The April LFS showing unemployment at 6.9% and private sector contraction has weighed on CAD independently of the oil price. A resolution scenario removes the oil-price support at the same time that domestic fundamentals are providing limited CAD uplift, which argues for a modestly weaker CAD in the constructive scenario than a simple oil-price model would predict.</p>

<p>The Bank of Canada''s June 10 statement will be read partly through the lens of what happened June 4. If the BoC characterizes the ceasefire as a genuine shift in the inflation outlook rather than a fragile preliminary signal, it is effectively closing the door on the hike language from April 29. That is a meaningful constraint removal for the rate path, and one that bond markets will price into GoC 5-year yields within hours of the statement''s release.</p>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who follow geopolitical news are cautiously optimistic and slightly confused. The ceasefire feels like a turning point, but three months of prior ceasefire signals that did not hold have eroded confidence. The dominant client emotion is qualified hope: wanting to believe this one is different, aware that the previous three were not. Clients with energy holdings are watching their positions with a mix of relief at current prices and uncertainty about whether to take gains now or hold through a potential resolution. Clients without energy exposure feel less urgency but are watching the inflation and rate implications carefully.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The Lebanon ceasefire is the most meaningful development since the conflict started in terms of the diplomatic architecture. Iran had been saying for weeks that it would not negotiate on the broader issues while Israel was still operating in Lebanon. That obstacle has now been removed, at least on paper. What has not changed is the physical situation: the Strait of Hormuz is still closed and the oil supply disruption is still happening. The question for your portfolio is not whether this is good news, it clearly is, but whether it changes what we should actually own right now. My read is that it shifts the probability distribution toward resolution without confirming it. That argues for maintaining current positioning rather than making a large directional move in either direction, and being ready to act when the probability shifts further.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with above-benchmark energy equity exposure on the TSX. A constructive resolution scenario compresses those valuations; the timing and magnitude of that compression depends on how quickly the strait reopens. These clients need a clear exit framework, not just a hold signal.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with balanced mandates that have benefited from energy outperformance without a deliberate overweight. The rebalancing question becomes relevant as the probability of resolution rises.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients in cash or underweight equities who have been waiting for geopolitical clarity before re-entering. A base case of sustained negotiation at reduced oil prices is a more stable entry environment than the peak-escalation conditions of early May.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review energy sector weights across all client books and document the current overweight or underweight relative to benchmark.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For clients with above-benchmark energy exposure, prepare a scenario-based discussion of at what oil price level their position thesis changes.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Monitor Trump''s weekend negotiations commentary: he signalled progress could be achieved "as early as this weekend." Any formal negotiating statement between now and Monday changes the base case probability materially.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review CAD-denominated foreign holdings for clients with significant US equity exposure: a resolution scenario has a specific CAD implication that affects the currency hedge calculus.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Lebanon ceasefire: what it changes and what it does not<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Yesterday''s ceasefire between Israel and Lebanon is genuinely meaningful, and I want to give you a clear picture of what it does and does not change for your portfolio.<br><br>
What it changes: Iran had been refusing to resume broader negotiations while Israel was operating in Lebanon. That obstacle has now been removed. President Trump said Wednesday that progress in US-Iran talks could come "as early as this weekend." The probability of a broader resolution has improved, and oil prices fell roughly 3% on that signal.<br><br>
What it does not change yet: the Strait of Hormuz is still closed. The physical oil supply disruption that has driven energy prices higher since March is still in place. We have seen four prior ceasefire signals since March; each produced a temporary price dip before renewed tensions. This one has a better structural foundation, but it has not yet been tested.<br><br>
My current read is that this argues for holding existing positions rather than making large directional moves, while being prepared to act if the negotiating progress over the next few days confirms a more durable shift. I will be in touch with specific recommendations for your situation as the picture develops.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors in Canadian energy names:</strong> This group has been sitting on significant gains since March without a professional framework for assessing when those gains are at risk. The ceasefire is the moment they most need a second opinion, before they either sell too early on the relief rally or hold through a resolution that compresses valuations.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with US trade exposure:</strong> Canadian exporters who have been navigating both the tariff uncertainty and the energy shock simultaneously have a complex risk picture. The geopolitical resolution scenario removes one layer of uncertainty while leaving the USMCA review risk intact.</p>
<p><strong>Investors holding international equity funds with Middle East or energy sector concentration:</strong> This segment has direct exposure to the resolution scenario and may not have a clear picture of what their fund manager''s positioning implies for their portfolio in the different scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling because yesterday''s Lebanon ceasefire is being read by markets as the most significant diplomatic development since the conflict started, and I wanted to make sure you had a clear picture of what it actually means for your positions before the weekend, when the next round of US-Iran negotiations is reportedly scheduled.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The difference between a probability shift and a resolution is the entire question right now, and it is a distinction that oil market commentary, financial news headlines, and trading app notifications cannot make for an individual investor. The ceasefire moved prices 3%. A full resolution would move them 15% to 20% in the opposite direction from current levels. A collapse would spike them 10% to 15% higher. The investor who does not have a framework for these three scenarios and their portfolio implications is making a decision by default, which is itself a decision with consequences.</p>
<p>That framework is exactly what an advisor provides. Not a prediction. A structure for acting correctly across the range of outcomes rather than reacting emotionally to the most recent headline.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How has your energy exposure performed over the last three months, and are you happy with where those positions stand today?</p>
<p>When you think about the ceasefire news, is your instinct to take some gains off the table, hold, or add more?</p>
<p>Do you have a clear sense of at what oil price level your energy positions would no longer make sense to hold?</p>
<p>Are you tracking the US-Iran negotiations directly, or are you relying on headlines to tell you when something significant has changed?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Lebanon ceasefire and your energy positions<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
Yesterday''s ceasefire between Israel and Lebanon moved oil prices roughly 3% lower and raised the probability that a broader US-Iran deal reopens the Strait of Hormuz. President Trump indicated negotiations could advance as early as this weekend.<br><br>
If that happens, Brent could retreat toward the $80 to $88 range from current levels near $95. For investors who have been holding Canadian energy names through the conflict period, that is a meaningful valuation shift that requires a clear framework for when and how to respond.<br><br>
The ceasefire is a probability shift, not a resolution. The strait is still closed. But the probability distribution has changed, and portfolios built for the prior regime may need adjustment as the new one takes shape.<br><br>
I would be glad to walk through the scenario analysis and what it means for your specific holdings.<br><br>
[Your Name] | [Firm] | [Phone]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',

