  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors who have been riding the energy rally without a plan:</strong> This group has significant unrealized gains in Canadian energy names and no framework for deciding when to reduce exposure. The ceasefire is the first credible resolution signal in three months and the moment they most need professional guidance.</p>
<p><strong>Investors in equity-heavy RRSP portfolios approaching retirement:</strong> Clients within five to seven years of retirement who have benefited from TSX energy outperformance now face a sequence-of-returns question: do they lock in gains before a potential resolution-driven compression, or maintain equity exposure through the resolution?</p>
<p><strong>Recent equity market entrants who bought Canadian names during the March volatility:</strong> Investors who added Canadian equity exposure during the March-April drawdown period are sitting on gains and have no cost basis context for current valuation levels.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling because between the jobs number this morning, the ceasefire yesterday, and the Bank of Canada on Wednesday, the next five days are the most information-dense period the TSX has seen in months, and I wanted to make sure you had a framework for thinking about your Canadian equity positions before the picture becomes clearer.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The TSX energy premium that has driven the index since March was earned by holding through significant volatility across four ceasefire cycles. The question now is not whether that premium was warranted: it was. The question is whether the portfolio that was right for a disruption environment is still right as the probability of resolution rises. Those are different portfolios, and the transition between them requires a deliberate decision, not a default.</p>
<p>The investor who built energy exposure in March and holds it indefinitely is making an implicit bet that the disruption continues. The investor who reduces energy exposure at the first ceasefire signal may give back gains on a reversal. The advisor''s value is in constructing the framework for that transition in advance, so that neither emotional response, hold everything or sell everything, drives the outcome.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How have your Canadian equity positions performed since the beginning of the year, and are you comfortable with the current mix of sector exposure?</p>
<p>Do you have a view on how long the current Hormuz disruption will last, and how that view is reflected in your portfolio?</p>
<p>If oil prices fell 15% to 20% over the next two months on a ceasefire resolution, how would that affect your overall financial plan?</p>
<p>Are you planning to make any portfolio changes ahead of the Bank of Canada decision on Wednesday?</p>
</div>
